#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186203777 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "0be03329-844c-5231-a459-314a1182f84b" :knowledge-point/title "Computing One Iteration of the Newton-Raphson Method"}] :question/id #uuid "1c25c6d9-21b9-59a5-a563-25cb89788286" :question/math-academy-id "e-16706" :question/problem "Suppose we wish to find a critical point of the function $f(x,y)$ in the region $[0,1] \\times [0,1] \\subseteq \\mathbb{R}^2$, where\n$f(x,y) = 2x^2y-2x^2+3xy^2+3xy+1$.\nCalculate one iteration of the Newton-Raphson process for optimization that starts at $\\mathbf x_0 = \\big[1, \\: 1 \\big]^T$. Round your final answer to three decimal places (if needed)." :question/worked-solution "The Newton-Raphson method of optimizing a multivariable function $f: \\mathbb{R}^n \\to \\mathbb{R}$ is\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{x}_{n+1} = \\mathbf{x}_n - [H_f(\\mathbf{x}_n)]^{-1} \\nabla{f}(\\mathbf{x}_n)\n$$\n\nwhere $H(\\mathbf{x}_n)$ and $\\nabla{f}(\\mathbf{x}_n)$ are the Hessian and gradient of $f$, respectively, evaluated at $\\mathbf{x}_n$.\n\nIn our case, the gradient is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n∇f(x) &= \\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{∂}{∂x}(2x^{2}y - 2x^{2} + 3xy^{2} + 3xy + 1) \\\\ \\frac{∂}{∂y}(2x^{2}y - 2x^{2} + 3xy^{2} + 3xy + 1)\\end{bmatrix} \\\\\n&= \\begin{bmatrix}4xy - 4x + 3y^{2} + 3y \\\\ 2x^{2} + 6xy + 3x\\end{bmatrix}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand the Hessian is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nH_{f}(x) &= \\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{∂}{∂x}(4xy - 4x + 3y^{2} + 3y) & \\frac{∂}{∂x}(2x^{2} + 6xy + 3x) \\\\ \\frac{∂}{∂y}(4xy - 4x + 3y^{2} + 3y) & \\frac{∂}{∂y}(2x^{2} + 6xy + 3x)\\end{bmatrix} \\\\\n&= \\begin{bmatrix}4y - 4 & 4x + 6y + 3 \\\\ 4x + 6y + 3 & 6x\\end{bmatrix}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our Newton-Raphson formula is\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}4y - 4 & 4x + 6y + 3 \\\\ 4x + 6y + 3 & 6x\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nStarting with\n$\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx_{1} &= x_{0} - \\begin{bmatrix}4y - 4 & 4x + 6y + 3 \\\\ 4x + 6y + 3 & 6x\\end{bmatrix}_{x_{0}}^{-1}\\begin{bmatrix}4xy - 4x + 3y^{2} + 3y \\\\ 2x^{2} + 6xy + 3x\\end{bmatrix}_{x_{0}} \\\\\n&= \\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix} - \\begin{bmatrix}4(1) - 4 & 4(1) + 6(1) + 3 \\\\ 4(1) + 6(1) + 3 & 6(1)\\end{bmatrix}^{-1}\\begin{bmatrix}4(1)(1) - 4(1) + 3(1)^{2} + 3(1) \\\\ 2(1)^{2} + 6(1)(1) + 3(1)\\end{bmatrix} \\\\\n&= \\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix} - \\begin{bmatrix}0 & 13 \\\\ 13 & 6\\end{bmatrix}^{-1}\\begin{bmatrix}6 \\\\ 11\\end{bmatrix} \\\\\n&= \\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix} - \\frac{1}{0 \\cdot 6 - 13 \\cdot 13}\\begin{bmatrix}6 & -13 \\\\ -13 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}6 \\\\ 11\\end{bmatrix} \\\\\n&= \\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix} + \\frac{1}{169}\\begin{bmatrix}-107 \\\\ -78\\end{bmatrix} \\\\\n&\\approx \\begin{bmatrix}0.367 \\\\ 0.538\\end{bmatrix}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186203778 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "8a19923a-5050-5417-8c83-5fc085a81f0f" :knowledge-point/title "Computing Two Iterations of the Newton-Raphson Method"}] :question/id #uuid "a5e04738-9b21-56d1-b483-703b82e3379a" :question/math-academy-id "e-16707" :question/problem "Suppose we wish to find a critical point of the function $f(x,y)$ in the region $[1,2] \\times [1,2] \\subseteq \\mathbb{R}^2$, where\n$f(x,y) = x^3+y^3-3x-6y+9$.\nCalculate the second iteration of the Newton-Raphson process for optimization that starts at $\\mathbf x_0 = \\big[1, \\: 2 \\big]^T$ if the first iteration of the algorithm gives $\\mathbf x_1 = \\big[1, \\: 1.5 \\big]^T$. Round your final answer to three decimal places (if needed)." :question/worked-solution "The Newton-Raphson method of optimizing a multivariable function $f: \\mathbb{R}^n \\to \\mathbb{R}$ is\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{x}_{n+1} = \\mathbf{x}_n - [H_f(\\mathbf{x}_n)]^{-1} \\nabla{f}(\\mathbf{x}_n)\n$$\n\nwhere $H(\\mathbf{x}_n)$ and $\\nabla{f}(\\mathbf{x}_n)$ are the Hessian and gradient of $f$, respectively, evaluated at $\\mathbf{x}_n$.\n\nIn our case, the gradient is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n∇f(x) &= \\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{∂}{∂x}(x^{3} + y^{3} - 3x - 6y + 9) \\\\ \\frac{∂}{∂y}(x^{3} + y^{3} - 3x - 6y + 9)\\end{bmatrix} \\\\\n&= \\begin{bmatrix}3x^{2} - 3 \\\\ 3y^{2} - 6\\end{bmatrix}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand the Hessian is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nH_{f}(x) &= \\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{∂}{∂x}(3x^{2} - 3) & \\frac{∂}{∂x}(3y^{2} - 6) \\\\ \\frac{∂}{∂y}(3x^{2} - 3) & \\frac{∂}{∂y}(3y^{2} - 6)\\end{bmatrix} \\\\\n&= \\begin{bmatrix}6x & 0 \\\\ 0 & 6y\\end{bmatrix}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our Newton-Raphson formula is\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}6x & 0 \\\\ 0 & 6y\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nFor the second iteration, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx_{2} &= x_{1} - \\begin{bmatrix}6x & 0 \\\\ 0 & 6y\\end{bmatrix}_{x_{1}}^{-1}\\begin{bmatrix}3x^{2} - 3 \\\\ 3y^{2} - 6\\end{bmatrix}_{x_{1}} \\\\\n&= \\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1.5\\end{bmatrix} - \\begin{bmatrix}6(1) & 0 \\\\ 0 & 6(1.5)\\end{bmatrix}^{-1}\\begin{bmatrix}3(1)^{2} - 3 \\\\ 3(1.5)^{2} - 6\\end{bmatrix} \\\\\n&= \\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1.5\\end{bmatrix} - \\begin{bmatrix}6 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 9\\end{bmatrix}^{-1}\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 0.75\\end{bmatrix} \\\\\n&= \\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1.5\\end{bmatrix} - \\frac{1}{6 \\cdot 9 - 0 \\cdot 0}\\begin{bmatrix}9 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 6\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 0.75\\end{bmatrix} \\\\\n&= \\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1.5\\end{bmatrix} - \\frac{1}{54}\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 4.5\\end{bmatrix} \\\\\n&\\approx \\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1.417\\end{bmatrix}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186203779 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "e3db0791-f73d-55e7-9b5a-f8faa62b8820" :knowledge-point/title "Computing Iterations of the Newton-Raphson Method Using Linear Systems"}] :question/id #uuid "e5c61c4c-52a7-52cb-85fe-bc9c3d5e49ae" :question/math-academy-id "e-16708" :question/problem "Suppose we wish to find a critical point of the function $f(x,y)$ in the region $[0,1]\\times[0,1] \\subseteq \\mathbb{R}^2$, where\n$f(x,y) = xy^2+3x^2-xy-x$.\nFind the matrix equation that defines the first iteration of the Newton-Raphson process starting at the point $\\big[1, \\: 1 \\big]^T$, and compute the components of the first iteration" :question/worked-solution "The Newton-Raphson method of optimizing a multivariable function $f: \\mathbb{R}^n \\to \\mathbb{R}$ is\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{x}_{n+1} = \\mathbf{x}_n - [H_f(\\mathbf{x}_n)]^{-1} \\nabla{f}(\\mathbf{x}_n)\n$$\n\nwhere $H(\\mathbf{x}_n)$ and $\\nabla{f}(\\mathbf{x}_n)$ are the Hessian and gradient of $f$, respectively, evaluated at $\\mathbf{x}_n$.\n\nEquivalently, at each iteration, the above equation can be rewritten as\n\n$$\nH_f(\\mathbf{x}_n)(\\mathbf{x}_{n+1} - \\mathbf{x}_n) = -\\nabla{f}(\\mathbf{x}_n)\n$$\n\nIn our case, the gradient is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n∇f(x) &= \\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{∂}{∂x}(xy^{2} + 3x^{2} - xy - x) \\\\ \\frac{∂}{∂y}(xy^{2} + 3x^{2} - xy - x)\\end{bmatrix} \\\\\n&= \\begin{bmatrix}y^{2} + 6x - y - 1 \\\\ 2xy - x\\end{bmatrix}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand the Hessian is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nH_{f}(x) &= \\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{∂}{∂x}(y^{2} + 6x - y - 1) & \\frac{∂}{∂x}(2xy - x) \\\\ \\frac{∂}{∂y}(y^{2} + 6x - y - 1) & \\frac{∂}{∂y}(2xy - x)\\end{bmatrix} \\\\\n&= \\begin{bmatrix}6 & 2y - 1 \\\\ 2y - 1 & 2x\\end{bmatrix}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our Newton-Raphson system of equations is\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}6 & 2y - 1 \\\\ 2y - 1 & 2x\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nStarting with $\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\begin{bmatrix}6 & 2y - 1 \\\\ 2y - 1 & 2x\\end{bmatrix}_{x_{0}}(x_{1} - x_{0}) &= -\\begin{bmatrix}y^{2} + 6x - y - 1 \\\\ 2xy - x\\end{bmatrix}_{x_{0}} \\\\\n\\begin{bmatrix}6 & 2(1) - 1 \\\\ 2(1) - 1 & 2(1)\\end{bmatrix}(x_{1} - x_{0}) &= -\\begin{bmatrix}(1)^{2} + 6(1) - (1) - 1 \\\\ 2(1)(1) - 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\\n\\begin{bmatrix}6 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{bmatrix}(x_{1} - x_{0}) &= \\begin{bmatrix}-5 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving this system of equations, we get $\\begin{bmatrix}-9/11 \\\\ -1/11\\end{bmatrix}$ Hence,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx_{1} &= x_{0} + \\begin{bmatrix}-9/11 \\\\ -1/11\\end{bmatrix} \\\\\n&= \\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix} + \\begin{bmatrix}-9/11 \\\\ -1/11\\end{bmatrix} \\\\\n&\\approx \\begin{bmatrix}0.182 \\\\ 0.909\\end{bmatrix}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186204458 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "0be03329-844c-5231-a459-314a1182f84b" :knowledge-point/title "Computing One Iteration of the Newton-Raphson Method"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/id #uuid "25e3de80-bb2f-5559-b5a3-362090da0ad2" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186202988}] :question/id #uuid "d38c1ba4-f0b4-54be-934d-689905d21c14" :question/math-academy-id "q-256562" :question/problem "Suppose we wish to find a critical point of the function $f(x, y)$ in the region $[0, 2] \\cdot [0, 2]⊆R^{2}$, where\n$f(x, y) = x^{2}y - 2x - y^{2} + 1$.\nCalculate one iteration of the Newton-Raphson process for optimization that starts at $x_{0} = [1, 1]^{T}$. Round your final answer to three decimal places (if needed).$[MATH: x_{1} = []$\n$1.25$\n$[MATH:]]$\n{{field-1}}"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186204459 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "e3db0791-f73d-55e7-9b5a-f8faa62b8820" :knowledge-point/title "Computing Iterations of the Newton-Raphson Method Using Linear Systems"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/id #uuid "b7053281-3b21-5fb0-8a65-bc903498a6ac" :answer-field/key "field-3" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186202989} {:answer-field/id #uuid "f08fef05-6bed-5a9c-9740-d6af450c785b" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186202990} {:answer-field/id #uuid "32c0b86b-9d32-5fda-9782-bba40b885de4" :answer-field/key "field-4" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186202991} {:answer-field/id #uuid "4710a21a-0b63-5b4e-90ad-e1b6780cbe88" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186202992}] :question/id #uuid "de703970-b156-5cd3-a172-a00a2a53e56f" :question/math-academy-id "q-256564" :question/problem "Suppose we wish to find a critical point of the function $f(x, y)$ in the region $[0, 1] \\cdot [0, 1]⊆R^{2}$, where\n$f(x, y) = 2x^{2}y - 4xy^{2} - x + 6y^{3}$.\nFill in the blanks in the matrix equation below that defines the first iteration of the Newton-Raphson process starting at the point $[1, 0]^{T}$, and compute the components of the first iteration, rounding your final answer to three decimal places (if needed).$[MATH: []$$0$\n{{field-1}}\n$[MATH:](x_{1} - x_{0}) = []$\n{{field-2}}\n$[MATH:]]$\n{{field-3}}\n$-8$$-2$\n$[MATH: x_{1} = []$$1$$[MATH:]]$\n{{field-4}}"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186204460 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "e3db0791-f73d-55e7-9b5a-f8faa62b8820" :knowledge-point/title "Computing Iterations of the Newton-Raphson Method Using Linear Systems"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/id #uuid "b8043409-855c-5c6d-a5d0-bf27f44cb3a7" :answer-field/key "field-3" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186202993} {:answer-field/id #uuid "efc0a149-8377-5d67-94e4-f2670f3cc2dd" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186202994} {:answer-field/id #uuid "f88f5792-3730-5e6e-a6ce-69fd98a48005" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186202995}] :question/id #uuid "cc5aadd2-f405-553a-a7ce-055d57e6d683" :question/math-academy-id "q-256567" :question/problem "Suppose we wish to find a critical point of the function $f(x, y)$ in the region $[0, 1] \\cdot [0, 1]⊆R^{2}$, where\n$f(x, y) = 5x^{2}y - 4x + 2y^{3} - 6y^{2}$.\nFill in the blanks in the matrix equation below that defines the first iteration of the Newton-Raphson process starting at the point $[1, 1]^{T}$.$[MATH: []$\n{{field-1}}\n$10$$[MATH:](x_{1} - x_{0}) = []$$-6$$[MATH:]]$$10$\n{{field-2}}\n\n{{field-3}}"}]}
